  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \nThe supplied algorithm uses SLSQP and Nelder-Mead to optimize an Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem. However, it struggles to escape local optima and does not systematically explore different scales or function structures.  \n**Central idea**: Use a hierarchical approach combining multi-resolution analysis, global optimization, and tailored initial states derived from theoretically optimal structures to find a candidate with lower C5.  \nKey improvements:\n- **Multi-resolution scaling**: Explore candidate functions at different sample lengths (n_candidates) to jump away from local optima.\n- **Enhanced initial states**: Use more optimal patterns (piecewise constant, sinusoidal, sparse) rather than random starts.\n- **Convex relaxation via surrogate**: Substitute the discontinuous penalty for a smooth surrogate (clipped exponential) to enforce constraints.\n- **Targeted optimization**: Balance gradient-based (SLSQP) and derivative-free methods (Nelder-Mead) based on problem structure.\n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n**Variables**:  \n- `n_candidates = [64, 128, 256, 512]` (powers of 2)\n- `h` vector of length `n_candidate` satisfying:  \n  - `\u2200i: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1`\n  - `\u2211h[i] = n_candidate/2`  \n**Objective**:  \n```\nC5(h, dx) = max_{k} \u222b h(x)\u00b7(1 - h(x+k)) dx  \n\u2248 max_lag [dx \u00b7 correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\")[lag]]\n```\n**Constraints**:  \n- **Equality**: `\u2211h[i] = n_candidate/2`\n- **Bounds**: `0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1`  \n**Derivative wrappings**:  \n- **Soft constraints**: Use `surrogate = log(1 + exp((\u2211h[i] - n_candidate/2)\u00b7\u03b2))` to emulate hard constraints.\n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n**Flowchart**:  \n```mermaid\ngraph TD\nA[Input Parent] --> B{Time Budget Query}\nB --> C{Generate & Validate Candidates}\nC --> D[Repair h]\nD --> E{Optimize using SLSQP/Nelder-Mead}\nE --> F[Local Improvement]\nF --> G[C5 Evaluation]\nG --> H{Global Timeout?}\nH -->|No| I[Select Best]\nH -->|Yes| J[Return Solution]\n```\n**Steps**:  \n1. Validate parent h (n=256).  \n2. For `n_candidate in [64, 128, 256, 512]`:  \n   a. Interpolate parent for `n_candidate`, if divisible.  \n   b. Generate starts: sinusoidal, piecewise, sparse, random.  \n   c. For each start, optimize:  \n      i. COBYLA with constraint `\u2211h[i] = n_candidate/2` (exact)  \n      ii. Local refinement with Nelder-Mead  \n   d. Apply repair: enforce bounds, rescale for \u2211h[i] (if needed).  \n3. Select candidate with minimal average C5 over lags.  \n\n4. Search choices  \n- **n_candidates**: Powers of 2 enable easy interpolation and cache reuse.  \n- **sinusoidal**: `h(x) = 0.5 + 0.5\u00b7sin(k\u00b7\u03c9x)` with amplitude optimization.  \n- **sparse**: Piecewise constant with sharp transitions.  \n- **hyperparameters**: SLSQP with \u03b2=100, Nelder-Mead steps=5000.  \n\n5. Budget and robustness  \n- **Time allocation**: 400s per n_candidate (total \u22481600s).  \n- **Repair**: Repair after each optimization step.  \n- **Fallback**: If timeout, return best-known valid candidate.  \n\n6. Validation  \n- Check `\u2211h[i] = n_candidate/2` to within 1e-5.  \n- C5 computed as `np.max(correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\"))*dx`.  \n- Output `(h, C5, n_candidate)` with C5 optimality.",
